<DocLayout
  title="Skills & MCP"
  description="SKILL.md packs, discovery paths, the composer skill picker, and MCP servers over stdio, HTTP, and SSE with tool permissions."
>
  <h2>Skills</h2>
  <p>
    A skill is a <code>SKILL.md</code> pack: prompt text that teaches the
    assistant a procedure. Skills are discovered from the opened project,
    your user config directory, <code>~/.agents/skills</code>, and a
    bundled set. Enable or disable them, inspect their content, and
    enabled skills are injected into the chat system prompt.
  </p>
  <p>
    The composer's <strong>Skill</strong> chip attaches one skill to a
    single message, and keyword auto-suggest can propose one from your
    draft. Skills are prompt text, not executed code — there are no
    per-skill permissions because a skill cannot run anything by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>MCP servers</h2>
  <p>
    Studio connects to Model Context Protocol servers over
    <strong>stdio</strong>, <strong>HTTP</strong>, and <strong>SSE</strong>.
    Tools are discovered automatically, validated against their schemas,
    and gated by permissions before the model can call them. The
    Connections view shows live status for each server.
  </p>

  <h2>How they differ</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skills</strong> change what the assistant <em>knows how to do</em> — instructions, injected as text.</li>
    <li><strong>MCP tools</strong> change what it <em>can call</em> — real capabilities, permission-gated.</li>
    <li><strong>Agents</strong> combine both inside the bounded run loop; see <a href={withBase('/docs/agents/')}>Agents &amp; runs</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
